# app agent2you

A distributable pack for deploying teams of chat-resident AI agents. Two halves:
the `a2y` python package (manifest loading, deterministic rendering, CLI) and
`a2y/image/` (the agent container: dockerfile, entrypoint, supervisord, Hermes
plugins), which `a2y init` vendors into each fleet workspace.

The design is extracted from a production fleet in the operator's `the source deployment`
repository (`the source fleet/`); that repo's `.hint` files carry the measurements
behind most rules here. When this pack and the source deployment disagree, check whether the source deployment
learned something new — or whether the pack generalised something the source deployment had
hardcoded.

# decision One agent is one container, and the stack inside is fixed {#one_container}

Hermes + litellm + acp2api + the coding CLIs, one image, one instance per
agent. Each layer exists because the others refuse to do its job: acp2api will
not fail over, litellm will not run a tool loop, Hermes will not speak ACP, and
the CLIs spend subscriptions through their own logins (no provider API key —
that is the LEGAL basis, not an optimisation). Do not split the layers into
shared services and do not "simplify" the two python virtualenvs into one
(Hermes exact-pins its tree; litellm pins ranges over the same packages).

# decision The stack is assembled per agent from the chain {#topology}

acp2api runs iff an ACP executor exists; litellm runs iff there is a failover
decision to make (`brains.litellm: auto` = chain of 2+; on/off override, YAML
booleans normalized); a single `kind: openai` brain is Hermes pointed straight
at the endpoint with neither process. The on-switch is FILE PRESENCE: the
entrypoint installs a supervisor section iff its config was rendered into
/config, and the compose healthcheck probes the topmost process that exists.
Do not add a parallel enable-flag mechanism — two sources of truth for what
runs is how they diverge. Chains are any length and per-agent; `kind: openai`
executors join a litellm chain as ordinary members. Guard rails, validated:
litellm off + chain>1 is an error, and an openai executor's api_key_env must
never be OPENAI_API_KEY (that name carries the litellm master key so no real
provider key can be billed by accident).

# decision Configs are generated, not hand-written {#render}

`a2y render` is deterministic: same manifests, same bytes. The deploy tree is
committed and reviewed; the manifests are the only input. Escape hatches, in
order: `hermes:`/`acp2api:` deep-merge keys in agent.yaml, then
`agents/<name>/overrides/<file>` verbatim replacement. When an override
repeats across fleets, teach the generator. Never make render read anything
outside the workspace, the clock, or randomness — determinism is the feature.

# decision Bridge network is the default; the shared namespace is opt-in {#network}

In bridge mode every agent keeps its own loopback, so all agents use the same
internal ports (10021/10022/10023/+8) and nothing is allocated. Port blocks,
resolv.conf repair and the fail-closed interface assertion exist ONLY for
`network.mode: container:<vpn>`. Do not reintroduce per-agent port bookkeeping
into the default path.

# decision The chat platform is a manifest option {#platform}

Hermes owns the adapters; the pack decides which env vars flow and which
plugins load. `mattermost` is the proven branch. The untagged-routing POLICY
(`a2y/image/plugins/untagged-routing/policy.py`) deliberately names no chat
product — a new platform adds a binding that answers the policy's two
questions, never a second copy of the rules.

# decision The bootloader is a prompt, and the supervisor owns the fleet repo {#bootstrap}

`a2y/bootstrap.md` (printed by `a2y bootstrap`, runnable without install via
`uvx agent2you bootstrap`) is the pack's entry point: a harness-agnostic
runbook that any coding agent executes to interview the operator and stand up
the first agent -- the SUPERVISOR -- into whatever infrastructure exists. It is
a prompt, not a script, on purpose: the variability it absorbs (platforms,
hosts, memory backends, brain mixes) is judgment work; everything deterministic
it delegates to `a2y` commands and verifies with real output.

The supervisor's contract, encoded in its soul by the bootloader: it OWNS the
fleet repository (single source of truth, nobody else edits it), it IS the
discovery authority (descriptions in manifests feed the roster and cards, so
owning the repo is owning "who does what"), and it HIRES via docs/hiring.md +
`a2y agent add`. Division of labor is absolute: the supervisor edits, renders
and commits INSIDE its container; image builds and container starts happen
OUTSIDE (operator or CI) -- no docker socket ever enters an agent. `a2y` is
baked into the image (AGENT2YOU_VERSION) for exactly this; when releasing,
remember that ARG must name a version that exists on PyPI.

# decision Agent creation is non-interactive on purpose {#agent_add}

`a2y agent add` takes everything as flags/JSON and prints a numbered next-steps
checklist. The INTERACTIVE layer is a fleet agent interviewing the operator in
chat (docs/hiring.md) and then making one tool call — do not add a TUI wizard;
that would put a second, worse conversation surface next to the one the fleet
already is. The command validates against the whole fleet and rolls back on
failure, so a bad call leaves no half-created agent.

# decision Releases are trusted-publishing, actions pinned by SHA {#release}

PyPI publishing uses OIDC trusted publishing: no API token exists anywhere, so
there is nothing for a compromised dependency to steal — the lesson of the
litellm 1.82.x incident, where the victim's own publish pipeline was the way
in. GitHub Actions are referenced by commit SHA with the tag in a comment;
bumping one means resolving the new tag's COMMIT sha (annotated tags point at a
tag object — dereference it), never trusting the tag name. The release
workflow gates on the test suite and on tag == pyproject version.

# decision A toolkit bundles install with usage {#toolkits}

`toolkits/<name>/` = `toolkit.yaml` (apt/npm/uv_tools/env + verbatim
`dockerfile:` escape hatch) + `USAGE.md` appended to the SOUL.md of every agent
carrying it. Fleet-level toolkits go into the fleet image; agent-level ones
produce a derived image `<tag>-<agent>` from a GENERATED dockerfile in
deploy/build/, built by `a2y build` in a chain (base -> fleet -> per-agent).
The pairing is the point: a tool and the instructions for it must not be able
to drift apart, and neither may live in a volume. Do not add tools to agents
any other way (hand-edited compose images, exec-time installs) — an install at
runtime is the ai-router mistake the one-container decision buried.

# decision A SOUL.md is short, and conduct is shared {#soul_philosophy}

A soul carries identity, scope, non-goals and agent-specific rules ONLY.
Fleet-wide conduct lives in SOUL-shared.md (rendered into everyone), tool
instructions in toolkit USAGE, operational knowledge in .hint files. Reasons,
all evidenced: every soul line is resent on every turn; long personas
measurably spend MORE reasoning tokens fighting themselves (the honey
measurement, and a 1400-line-persona report of 10x token burn); and vague
lines ("you have a tone") or grammatically broken bullets read as noise or
mislead the model. Write complete imperative sentences. The shared conduct
includes two rules born from measured fleet failures: self-reporting is not
evidence, and invoke-before-denying (enumeration is narration). Do not grow
skeletons back into personality essays, and do not put delegation matrices or
reasoning-loop rituals into souls -- routing is structural (untagged-routing,
roster) and the coding CLIs run their own loops.

# invariant example.env is complete {#env_parity}

The generated `example.env` names every variable the compose file references,
and `a2y doctor` enforces `.env` ⊇ `example.env`. A missing variable can make
an UNRELATED service's compose fail to render; this invariant is what keeps
that class of failure extinct.

# bad Traps already paid for — do not reintroduce {#traps}

* cline self-updates from `latest` at startup unless `CLINE_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1`
  is in the environment; the render always sets it. Removing it turns the
  image pin into fiction inside a container full of OAuth logins.
* `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=/root/.claude` is load-bearing: without it Claude Code
  keeps half its state outside every volume and a container replacement loses
  the account while appearing to keep it.
* codex's sandbox is set per TURN by the ACP session `mode:` in acp2api.yaml
  (`agent-full-access`); `sandbox_mode` in config.toml governs only the human
  CLI. Believing the config.toml fix cost the source deployment two rounds.
* `bypassPermissions` is refused when Claude Code runs as root (these
  containers do); `acceptEdits` is the correct mode and costs nothing on top
  of `permission: allow`.
* Never put a real provider key in `OPENAI_API_KEY` — codex may prefer it and
  quietly bill the API instead of spending the subscription.
* `warmup` in acp2api stays off: a forked session's MCP servers are dead
  (measured 2026-08-14) — the agent can name its tools and cannot call one.
* Anything that prints from bashrc must be guarded with `[ -t 1 ]`: the coding
  agent sources it for every Bash call, and an unguarded banner lands in its
  context on every command.
* stdio MCP servers: `env:` REPLACES the child's environment (node falls off
  PATH); pass URLs as flags, not env, when a package reads several aliases.
* A monkeypatched Hermes method must present the wrapped signature — a
  `**kwargs` wrapper advertises parameters the real method lacks and every
  call dies in a caught-and-logged handler (trace-to-card, weeks, invisibly).

# good Version discipline in the image {#pins}

Every tool is an ARG pin; raw binaries are checksum-verified against the
publisher's sums; npm installs use `--include=optional` AND the build asserts
the Claude Agent SDK platform binary landed (a miss fails every request with a
content-free `-32603`). Bumping `LITELLM_VERSION` requires checking
`fastapi.dependencies.utils.get_flat_dependant` still exists or adjusting
`FASTAPI_VERSION`.

# build

`uv run --group dev pytest` is the gate — it covers render determinism (two
runs, identical bytes), loud validation, the env-parity invariant, override
replacement, shared-namespace ports, and `agent add` rollback. `uv build` must
keep shipping `a2y/image/**` as package data (the wheel is what `a2y init`
vendors from); a change that drops it breaks init only at the user's machine.
Full smoke: init a workspace, render, `docker compose config -q` with a filled
`.env`.
